<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flow" target="russia_ukraine_war::defense_market_sentiment" id="0">
  <data key="d27">intra_causal</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flow" target="russia_ukraine_war::negotiation_hardening" id="0">
  <data key="d27">intra_causal</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flow" target="ai_buildout_and_bust::capital_flow_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained high volumes of external military assistance reallocates public budgetary capacity and shifts private investor demand toward defense suppliers while raising geopolitical risk premia, thereby reducing net capital flowing into AI investments and infrastructure.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_buildout_and_bust</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flow" target="iran_gulf_conflict::shipping_disruption_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained increases in Western military deliveries to Ukraine force redeployment or prioritization of naval and airlift assets toward Europe and away from Gulf security missions, reducing on‑station deterrence and enabling more attacks/inspections that disrupt merchant shipping.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flow" target="tech_trade_and_chip_riva::crossborder_capital_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained rises in external military aid raise perceived geopolitical and regulatory risk for investors and trigger tighter foreign investment screening and voluntary divestments in sensitive tech sectors, reducing cross‑border M&amp;A and investment flows.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">tech_trade_and_chip_riva</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flow" target="us_political_realignment::legislative_control_bala" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Larger, recurring congressional funding packages for Ukraine make aid a high-profile vote that exposes and deepens intra-majority splits and increases incumbent vulnerability in swing districts, narrowing the governing coalition's legislative margin during the next election cycle.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_political_realignment</data>
</edge>
